            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Unmerited favor, specifically God's free and unearned gift of salvation, forgiveness, and love toward humanity despite human sinfulness.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Ephesians 2:8-9\",\"Romans 3:24\",\"Romans 11:6\",\"Titus 2:11\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Divine empowerment, an enabling presence, or spiritual strength given to believers for sanctification, endurance, and ministry.\",\"basis\":[\"2 Corinthians 12:9\",\"1 Corinthians 15:10\",\"Hebrews 4:16\"],\"prominence\":\"dominant\"},{\"meaning\":\"Covenant loyalty and steadfast love, often conceptually overlapping with the Hebrew term 'chesed'.\",\"basis\":[\"Exodus 34:6\",\"John 1:14\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Physical attractiveness, aesthetic charm, elegance, or general relational favor in the eyes of others.\",\"basis\":[\"Proverbs 31:30\",\"Luke 2:40\",\"Luke 4:22\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While Protestant traditions historically emphasize 'unmerited favor' regarding justification, Catholic, Orthodox, and Wesleyan traditions strongly emphasize grace as 'divine empowerment' (sanctifying or actual grace). Both concepts represent deeply settled, widespread consensus within biblical theology.\"}",
